Protection Share Under Observable Behavior: Reachability-Conditional Information, Optimization-Course Effects, and the Limits of Trace Hiding

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 


Abstract

Trace hiding is the practice of withholding a teacher's exact reasoning traces R while exposing a visible channel V—final answers, short summaries, or action trajectories. The protection question is not whether hiding R reduces some undifferentiated quantity of capability transfer, but what residual capability transfer remains attributable to R beyond V, and how that residual depends on training configuration, student architecture, and inference budget B. We formalize the problem through the protection headroom H_{\mathcal{S}}=G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S})-G(V), measured against the student-class oracle ceiling rather than the teacher, and the protection share \eta, defined only when the headroom is positive. We decompose \eta=\eta_{\mathrm{info}}+\eta_{\mathrm{opt}} with a signed optimization-course component, because semantically coherent rationales misaligned with the student's discriminative objective can reduce capability below V-only training. The information-theoretic bound on \eta_{\mathrm{info}} is attenuated by two reachability factors: structural accessibility \alpha, the projection of the R-induced gradient onto the student's structurally reachable subspace, and novelty \nu, its component orthogonal to the span of V-supervision. The resulting bound

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}\le \alpha\nu\,\frac{I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)}{I(V,R;\Theta)}

is complemented by a local quadratic lower bound under a Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition, \Delta G_{\mathrm{info}}\ge \alpha^2\nu^2\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2/(2\mu), so that information contributes at most linearly and, under local strong convexity, at least quadratically in \alpha\nu. A Gaussian calibration with a non-commuting reachable subspace yields a closed-form \eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}} whose denominator is the reachable dimension k=\dim\mathcal{P}, and whose Schur-complement penalty quantifies the degradation induced by coupling to unobservable directions. The non-monotone budget interaction is characterized by a pass@K crossing at budget B^*. A one-sided Le Cam deficiency yields an upper bound on \eta_{\mathrm{info}}(\lambda) for a leakage-parameterized visible channel, and a posterior-predictive null R_{\mathrm{null}}\sim P(R\mid X,V) makes the decomposition exact with zero interaction residual. Two components are anchored in external empirical results—the reachability gap in low-rank distillation and the pass@K reversal in on-policy distillation test-time scaling; the remainder are formal propositions under stated assumptions or proposed identification strategies. The central empirical prediction is that in action-rich visible channels the residual protection attributable to exact traces beyond the best visible-only architecture is small or zero, and that the interesting residual, when it exists, is an optimization-course phenomenon.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Protection Question

A teacher model exposes two channels to a student: a visible channel V, typically the final answer, a short summary, or an action trajectory, and exact traces R, typically the full reasoning sequence. Trace hiding withholds R while publishing V, under the assumption that the visible channel carries less transferable capability than the exact traces. Recent results complicate this assumption. When the visible channel contains action trajectories, students trained without R can match or exceed students trained with full reasoning supervision on agentic benchmarks. The question is therefore not whether hiding R reduces some undifferentiated quantity of capability transfer, but:

Given a visible channel V of variable richness, what residual capability transfer remains attributable to R beyond V, and how does that residual depend on the training configuration, the student architecture, and the inference budget B?

We formalize the answer around two quantities. The protection headroom is

H_{\mathcal{S}} = G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S})-G(V),

where G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S})=\sup_{\phi\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{S}}}G(\phi) is the best capability attainable by the student class \mathcal{S} under any supervision, and G(V) is the capability attainable from V under the best available training procedure. The headroom is measured against the student-class oracle rather than the teacher G_T, because a student class may be unable to reach the teacher's capability even with oracle traces; the teacher-relative quantity H=G_T-G(V) is retained only as an upper reference. The protection share is

\eta=\frac{G(V,R)-G(V)}{G(V,R)-G(\varnothing)},

defined only when the denominator is non-vanishing. Protection share is meaningful only when H_{\mathcal{S}}>0; if G(V) approaches G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S}), the denominator of any normalized protection ratio vanishes and \eta becomes unstable or undefined.

1.2 Why Information Alone Does Not Settle the Question

The natural first attempt is to bound \eta by an information-theoretic ratio: if R contains little information about the latent procedure \Theta beyond what (X,V) already determines, then hiding R should protect. This attempt fails for a reason independent of estimation fragility: information is necessary but not sufficient for capability transfer, because the gradient updates induced by supervising on R must lie in directions the optimizer can reach. The two components of the failure are distinct.

The first is structural reachability. A distiller may deploy a full-width student but tie training to a teacher-induced subspace, leaving a large fraction of each deployed matrix's independent linear degrees of freedom unreachable to the optimizer; enlarging the training object to the entire deployed matrix, with no change in deployed shape, parameter count, or inference FLOPs, recovers capability at fixed deployed size. Two students with identical deployed architectures and identical supervision can therefore have different capability because their training trajectories span different subspaces. Any information-theoretic bound that ignores this structural constraint is not a bound on the trained student.

The second is optimization-course effects. A joint teacher–student objective that trains an unconditioned student against a privileged-information-conditioned teacher, using action-only trajectories, can match or outperform pipelines that assume full reasoning supervision. Conversely, rationales that are semantically coherent and expert-validated can hurt performance relative to label-only fine-tuning when used as training targets rather than inference-time demonstrations, and the harm is not explained by rationale quality but by a structural conflict between narrative plausibility and the gradient signal the student actually needs. The sign and magnitude of G(V,R)-G(V) are therefore governed by optimization geometry at least as much as by informational content.

The inference budget adds a third complication. On-policy distillation can improve pass@K at both small and large sampling budgets, or improve small-budget performance while reducing large-budget pass@K, with a reversal condition that holds even when the teacher is more accurate on every problem. The budget interaction is therefore not monotone in general. Finally, leakage is a design variable rather than a limitation: interface-level concealment does not prevent recovery of useful reasoning supervision, because in-context demonstrations wrapped in auxiliary formats can raise user-visible traces while preserving reasoning signals. The protection share is a family \eta(\lambda) indexed by a leakage level, not a single number.

1.3 The Reachability Turn

These four observations jointly motivate the central move of this paper: the information-theoretic bound on the informational component must be attenuated by reachability factors, and the optimization-course component must be signed and identified separately.

Let \mathcal{P}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d be the structurally reachable subspace of the student, fixed by architecture and initialization. Define

\alpha = \mathbb{E}_t\!\left[\frac{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|}{\|g_R\|}\right],
\qquad
\nu = \mathbb{E}_t\!\left[\frac{\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V_t})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|}{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|}\right],

where g_R is the gradient direction induced by R-supervision, \Pi_{\mathcal{P}} projects onto the structurally reachable subspace, and \mathcal{K}^V_t is the span of V-supervised gradients at step t within \mathcal{P}. Accessibility \alpha measures whether R's gradient directions lie inside what the architecture and initialization permit; novelty \nu measures the component of the accessible R-gradient orthogonal to what V already spans. The reachability-conditional bound is

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}\le \alpha\nu\,\frac{I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)}{I(V,R;\Theta)}.

When \alpha\approx 0, information in R cannot be converted into capability regardless of I(R;\Theta\mid X,V). When \nu\approx 0, R's accessible content is redundant with V. The product \alpha\nu is a multiplicative attenuation of the mutual-information ratio.

The decomposition

\eta=\eta_{\mathrm{info}}+\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}, \qquad \eta_{\mathrm{opt}}\in\mathbb{R},

allows the optimization-course component to be negative, because semantically coherent rationales can reduce capability relative to V-only training. The two components are identified through a provably neutral null: a trace sampled from the conditional P(R\mid X,V) carries zero additional information about \Theta given (X,V) by the data-processing inequality, yet preserves the conditional format and length of the true trace. The resulting decomposition telescopes exactly, with zero interaction residual.

1.4 Contributions

The paper makes the following contributions.

1. Reachability-conditional information bound. We replace the unconditional mutual-information bound on the protection share with \eta_{\mathrm{info}}\le\alpha\nu\cdot I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)/I(V,R;\Theta), where \alpha is structural accessibility and \nu is novelty relative to V. We argue that neither factor can be inferred from deployed parameter count, and that the product structure separates the question "could the optimizer reach R's directions?" from "does R add anything V does not already span?".
2. Closed-form Gaussian calibration with non-commuting reachability. Under A_V-invariance of \mathcal{P} alone, we derive

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}
=
\frac{\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)}{k-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)},

with

\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}
=
\Big(I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}
-A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}(I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp})^{-1}A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}}\Big)^{-1}.

The denominator is the reachable dimension k=\dim\mathcal{P}, not the ambient d, and the Schur-complement penalty is negative semidefinite: coupling between \mathcal{P} and unobservable nuisance directions in \mathcal{P}^{\perp} acts as correlated observation noise and degrades the information share below the commuting case.

3. Quadratic risk lower bound under local strong convexity. At a V-stationary point \phi_V^* where the joint risk \mathcal{L}_{VR} is \mu-strongly convex and L-smooth on \mathcal{P}, a projected gradient step and the Polyak–Łojasiewicz inequality give

G(V,R)-G(V)\ge \frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2L}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2,
\qquad
\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)\ge \frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2\mu}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

Information contributes at most linearly in \alpha\nu and, under local strong convexity, at least quadratically. The two bounds together bracket the informational component.

4. Pass@K crossing and a pre-computable budget predictor. For a partition of the task distribution into modal and tail tasks, the limiting large-K advantage of trace supervision is the tail mass \pi_{\mathrm{tail}}, and the crossover budget is

B^*\approx \frac{1}{\epsilon}\ln\!\left(\frac{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}}{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}\right),

where \epsilon is the tail success rate. A trace-versus-visible advantage score computed before training predicts the sign of d\eta/dB.

5. Le Cam deficiency bound and leakage parameterization. For a bounded loss, the oracle capability gap between the full and leaked experiments is bounded by M_\ell\,\delta(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}), giving

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}(\lambda)\le \alpha\nu\,\frac{M_\ell\,\delta(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR})}{G^{\mathrm{oracle}}_{\mathcal{S}}(V,R)-G_{\mathcal{S}}(\varnothing)}.

For an erasure channel the deficiency is linear, \delta=(1-\lambda)\delta_0, and Pinsker's inequality recovers the mutual-information bound as a special case.

6. Causal identification with a provably neutral null. A posterior-predictive null R_{\mathrm{null}}\sim P(R\mid X,V) satisfies I(R_{\mathrm{null}};\Theta\mid X,V)=0 identically by the Markov chain \Theta\to(X,V)\to R_{\mathrm{null}}. The resulting decomposition \eta=\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathrm{causal}}+\eta_{\mathrm{opt}}^{\mathrm{causal}} telescopes exactly with \eta_{\mathrm{int}}\equiv 0, isolating pure scaffolding gain and pure narrative–discriminative interference from the epistemic value of true trace content.

1.5 Status and Honesty

The claims in this paper have uneven epistemic standing, and we state the standing explicitly rather than allowing it to be inferred.

Two components are anchored in external empirical results: the reachability gap in low-rank distillation, which demonstrates that a large fraction of deployed linear degrees of freedom can be untrainable at fixed deployed shape, and the pass@K reversal in on-policy distillation test-time scaling, which demonstrates that finite-budget distillation can degrade large-budget performance even when the teacher dominates pointwise. These anchors supply the motivation for \alpha and for the non-monotone budget interaction, respectively.

Three components are formal propositions under stated assumptions: the Gaussian calibration (§3), which assumes a subspace-restricted Bayes-optimal estimator and A_V-invariance of \mathcal{P}; the quadratic risk bound (§4), which assumes local strong convexity and smoothness of the joint risk on \mathcal{P}; and the Le Cam deficiency bound (§6), which assumes a bounded loss and yields an upper bound that is O(\delta) and therefore loose. None of these is claimed to hold for a gradient-trained network without the stated assumptions, and the Gaussian calibration in particular is an idealized reference for an oracle restricted to \mathcal{P}, not a prediction for a trained student.

The posterior-predictive null (§7) is a proposed identification strategy. Its causal argument is exact conditional on the auxiliary generator not leaking \Theta; training such a generator without leakage remains an open implementation problem, and we specify an audit that a positive mutual-information estimate invalidates the null.

The paper makes no empirical claims. Experiments are specified but not executed; the dual-use constraint is explicit, and any future implementation is restricted to open-weight teachers and benign tasks.

1.6 The Central Empirical Prediction

Taken together, the framework yields a single empirical prediction that organizes the research program:

In regimes where the visible channel is action-rich, the residual protection attributable to exact traces beyond the best visible-only training architecture is small or zero; the interesting residual, when it exists, is an optimization-course phenomenon.

If this prediction holds, the protection story must be rewritten as a theory of optimization-course protection rather than informational protection, and the policy lever shifts from withholding traces to controlling the training architecture and the inference budget. If it fails—if trace supervision adds capability beyond the best visible-only architecture at some budget, in a pattern stable across budget and sensitive to trace verification—then the residual is informational and the reachability-corrected bound identifies the conditions under which it can be converted into capability.

1.7 Organization

Section 2 defines the primary quantities, the signed decomposition, and the estimation of the optimization component through format-matched nulls. Section 3 develops the reachability-conditional information bound and the Gaussian calibration with non-commuting reachability. Section 4 proves the quadratic risk lower bound under a Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition. Section 5 derives the pass@K crossing and the pre-computable budget predictor. Section 6 establishes the Le Cam deficiency bound and the leakage parameterization. Section 7 presents the posterior-predictive null and the exact telescoping decomposition. Section 8 specifies the estimation algorithms, the 2\times 2 factorial separating supervision from training objective, and the budget stratification. Appendices contain the full derivations, the supporting lemmas, the estimation protocols, and the audit procedure for the posterior-predictive null.

1. Problem and Primary Quantities

A teacher with latent procedure Θ emits a visible channel V and exact traces R. A student of class 𝒮 is trained under supervision arm A\in\{\varnothing,V,(V,R)\} on task distribution 𝒯 with training budget T and evaluated at inference budget B. Capability is G(A;\mathcal{T},\mathcal{S},T,B).

The protection headroom is

H_{\mathcal{S}} = G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S}) - G(V),

where G^{\mathrm{oracle}}(\mathcal{S})=\sup_{\phi\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{S}}}G(\phi) is the student-class oracle ceiling, not the teacher’s capability G_T. The teacher-relative quantity H=G_T-G(V) is retained only as an upper reference. The protection share is

\eta = \frac{G(V,R)-G(V)}{G(V,R)-G(\varnothing)},

defined when the denominator is non-vanishing. The signed decomposition is

\eta = \eta_{\mathrm{info}} + \eta_{\mathrm{opt}}, \qquad \eta_{\mathrm{opt}}\in\mathbb{R}.

The optimization component is allowed to be negative because semantically coherent rationales that are misaligned with the student’s discriminative objective can reduce capability relative to V-only training. \eta_{\mathrm{opt}} is estimated through a conditional null described in §7.

---

2. Reachability: The Structural Constraint

Let \mathcal{P}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d be the structurally reachable subspace of the student, fixed by architecture and initialization. The Low-Rank Clone distiller deploys a full-width student MLP but ties training to a teacher-induced slice, leaving 62.5\%\!-\!81.4\% of each deployed matrix’s independent linear degrees of freedom unreachable—paid for at inference, never trainable. Making the training object the entire deployed matrix, with no change in deployed shape, parameter count, or inference FLOPs, recovers +2.36/+2.71/+10.45 Avg9 across three teachers at fixed deployed shape. This is a direct demonstration that deployed capacity and trainable capacity can diverge sharply, and that enlarging the reachable set at fixed deployed size changes capability.

Define the accessibility and novelty factors

\alpha = \mathbb{E}_t\left[ \frac{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}} g_R\|}{\|g_R\|} \right],
\qquad
\nu = \mathbb{E}_t\left[ \frac{\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^{V}_t})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}} g_R\|}{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}} g_R\|} \right],

where g_R is the gradient direction induced by R-supervision, \Pi_{\mathcal{P}} projects onto the structurally reachable subspace, and \mathcal{K}^{V}_t is the span of V-supervised gradients at step t within \mathcal{P}. Accessibility measures whether R’s gradient directions lie inside what the architecture permits; novelty measures the component of accessible R-gradient orthogonal to what V already spans.

The reachability-conditional information bound is

\boxed{\;\eta_{\mathrm{info}} \;\le\; \alpha\,\nu\;\cdot\; \frac{I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)}{I(V,R;\Theta)}\;}

Since \alpha,\nu\le 1, the product is a multiplicative attenuation of the mutual-information ratio. When \alpha\approx 0, information in R cannot be converted into capability regardless of I(R;\Theta\mid X,V); when \nu\approx 0, R’s accessible content is redundant with V.

---

3. Gaussian Calibration with a Non-Commuting Reachable Subspace

Let \Theta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_d), G=-\operatorname{MMSE}, and let V and R contribute Fisher precisions A_V,A_R. Restrict the student to estimators supported on \mathcal{P}=\operatorname{range}(U), U\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}, and assume \mathcal{P} is A_V-invariant:

\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}A_V = A_V\Pi_{\mathcal{P}},
\qquad
A_V=\operatorname{diag}(A_{V,\mathcal{P}},A_{V,\perp})

in the block basis [U,U_{\perp}]. No commutativity with A_R is assumed; the cross-block coupling is A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}=U^{\top}A_R U_{\perp}.

The joint precision is J_{VR}=I_d+A_V+A_R. Applying the Schur complement to the \mathcal{P}-block gives

\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}
=
\Big(
I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}
-
A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}
\big(I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp}\big)^{-1}
A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}}
\Big)^{-1}.

Proposition 1 (Non-commuting Gaussian information share). Under A_V-invariance of \mathcal{P} alone,

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}
=
\frac{
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)
-
\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}{
k-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}.

Proof. Under the subspace restriction, the posterior mean is projected: \hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}}=\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\mu_{\mathrm{post}}, with \operatorname{MMSE}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})=\operatorname{tr}(\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}})+\operatorname{tr}(\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^{\perp}}\Sigma_0). Since \mathcal{P}^{\perp} is fixed at prior variance 1, the maximum reducible variance is k=\dim(\mathcal{P}), not d; hence the denominator k-\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}). The \mathcal{P}-block of \Sigma_{VR} follows from the Schur complement of J_{VR}. ∎

The Schur penalty

-A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}\big(I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp}\big)^{-1}A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}}\preceq 0

reduces effective precision inside \mathcal{P}: when A_R couples \mathcal{P} to unobservable nuisance directions in \mathcal{P}^{\perp}, uncertainty in \mathcal{P}^{\perp} acts as correlated observation noise and degrades \eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}. This is the exact mechanism by which non-commutativity attenuates the informational share even when I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)>0.

When \mathcal{P}=\mathbb{R}^d, the expression reduces to the full-space Gaussian reference. When \mathcal{P} excludes the directions in which A_R is informative, the trace difference vanishes and \eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}=0 despite positive full-space mutual information.

