4. Risk Lower Bound from Accessibility and Novelty

The upper bound \eta_{\mathrm{info}}\le\alpha\nu\cdot I(R;\Theta\mid X,V)/I(V,R;\Theta) is complemented by a local quadratic lower bound under a Polyak–Łojasiewicz (PL) condition.

Let \phi_V^* be a V-stationary point: \nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}_V(\phi_V^*)=0. Assume the joint risk \mathcal{L}_{VR} restricted to \mathcal{P} is \mu-strongly convex and L-smooth in a ball around \phi_V^*. Strong convexity implies the PL inequality

\frac{1}{2}\|\nabla f(x)\|^2 \ge \mu\big(f(x)-f^*\big),

which is the standard quadratic-growth condition used to establish linear convergence of gradient methods.

At \phi_V^*, the V-gradient vanishes, so g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)=g_R(\phi_V^*). Projecting onto \mathcal{P} and removing the V-gradient span \mathcal{K}^{V},

\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2
\ge
\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^{V}})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2
=
\alpha(\phi_V^*)\,\nu(\phi_V^*)\,\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2.

Proposition 2 (Quadratic risk-gap lower bound). At a V-stationary point where \mathcal{L}_{VR} is \mu-strongly convex and L-smooth on \mathcal{P}, a single projected gradient step \phi^+=\phi_V^*-\frac{1}{L}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*) satisfies

G(V,R)-G(V)
\ge
\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi^+)
\ge
\frac{1}{2L}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2
\ge
\frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2L}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

If \phi_{VR}^* is the \mathcal{P}-constrained minimizer of \mathcal{L}_{VR}, then

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)
\ge
\frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2\mu}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

Proof. The first inequality is the descent lemma under L-smoothness; the second is the PL inequality applied at \phi_V^*; the third substitutes the projection inequality above. ∎

The bound establishes quadratic scaling \Delta G_{\mathrm{info}}=\Omega(\alpha^2\nu^2) near V-stationarity, contrasting with the linear upper bound O(\alpha\nu). The two bounds together bracket the informational contribution: information can contribute at most linearly in \alpha\nu, and under local strong convexity it contributes at least quadratically.

---

5. Budget Interaction: Pass@K Crossing

The inference-budget dependence of \eta is non-monotone. On-policy distillation can improve pass@K at both small and large sampling budgets, or improve small-budget performance while reducing large-budget pass@K. The reversal occurs even when the teacher has higher accuracy on every problem, and a sufficient condition has been derived from how problem-level accuracy changes receive different weights as K grows.

For arm A with per-prompt success probability p_A(x),

G_K(A)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{T}}\Big[1-\big(1-p_A(x)\big)^K\Big].

Partition \mathcal{T} into modal tasks \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}=\{x:p_V(x)>p_{VR}(x)\} and tail tasks \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}=\{x:p_{VR}(x)>p_V(x)\approx 0\}. Let \pi_{\mathrm{tail}}=\mathbb{P}(x\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}) and \epsilon=\mathbb{E}[p_{VR}(x)\mid\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}].

Proposition 3 (Pass@K crossing).

\lim_{K\to\infty}\big(G_K(V,R)-G_K(V)\big)=\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}>0,

and the crossover G_{B^*}(V,R)=G_{B^*}(V) occurs at

\boxed{\;B^* \approx \frac{1}{\epsilon}\,
\ln\!\left(
\frac{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}}
{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}
\right).\;}

Proof. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}, (1-p_V)^K\to 0 while (1-p_{VR})^K\to 0 on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}; the limiting advantage is the tail mass \pi_{\mathrm{tail}}. The crossover solves (1-p_V)^K-(1-p_{VR})^K=0 in expectation, expanded to first order in the tail rate \epsilon. ∎

The Teacher Advantage Score at K (TAS@K) is computed before training and predicts which teacher yields larger pass@K improvement; across thirteen benchmarks its ordering agrees with observed improvements in 83.6\% of experiments. The protection-share analogue is a trace-versus-visible advantage score computed from the two output distributions, used to pre-register whether \eta(B) is expected to increase, flatten, or decrease across B.

