Appendix B: Gaussian Calibration with Non-Commuting Reachability

B.1 Posterior Covariance and the Subspace-Restricted Estimator

Let \Theta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_d). Under Gaussian observations with precision contributions A_V,A_R, the joint precision is

J_{VR}=I_d+A_V+A_R,\qquad \Sigma_{VR}=J_{VR}^{-1}.

The posterior mean is \mu_{\mathrm{post}}=\Sigma_{VR}b, where b is the sufficient statistic. An estimator constrained to output \hat{\theta}=Uw, w\in\mathbb{R}^k, minimizes

\mathbb{E}\|\Theta-Uw\|_2^2
=
\operatorname{tr}\Sigma_{VR}+\|Uw-\mu_{\mathrm{post}}\|_2^2

since the cross term vanishes at the posterior mean. The minimizer is w^*=U^\top\mu_{\mathrm{post}}, so

\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}}=\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\mu_{\mathrm{post}}.

B.2 MMSE Decomposition

Write \Theta=\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\Theta+\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^\perp}\Theta. The posterior is independent across the two blocks only under commutativity. In general,

\operatorname{MMSE}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
=
\mathbb{E}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\Theta-\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\mu_{\mathrm{post}}\|_2^2
+
\mathbb{E}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^\perp}\Theta\|_2^2.

The second term is fixed at prior variance because \mathcal{P}^\perp receives no update:

\mathbb{E}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^\perp}\Theta\|_2^2=\operatorname{tr}(\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^\perp})=d-k.

The first term is

\operatorname{tr}\big(\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\Sigma_{VR}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}\big)
=
\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big),

where \Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}=U^\top\Sigma_{VR}U. Hence

\operatorname{MMSE}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
=
\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)+(d-k).

Under V-only supervision, the analogous quantity is

\operatorname{MMSE}_V(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
=
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)+(d-k),

because A_V is block-diagonal in the [U,U_\perp] basis and the \mathcal{P}^\perp block remains at prior variance.

B.3 Proof of Proposition 1

Proposition 1. Under (SA1)–(SA2),

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}
=
\frac{
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)
-
\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}{
k-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}.

Proof. The information share is the fraction of reducible variance removed by R:

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}
=
\frac{
\operatorname{MMSE}_V(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
-
\operatorname{MMSE}_{VR}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
}{
\operatorname{MMSE}_V(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
-
\operatorname{MMSE}_{\mathrm{prior}}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
}.

The denominator is the total reducible variance under V-only supervision:

\operatorname{MMSE}_V(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})-\operatorname{MMSE}_{\mathrm{prior}}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})
=
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)+(d-k)-(d)
=
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)-k.

Wait—this is negative, because (I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\preceq I_k. The correct normalization uses the maximum reducible variance in \mathcal{P}, which is k, not d. The prior MMSE restricted to \mathcal{P} is k. Hence

\operatorname{MMSE}_V(\hat{\theta}_{\mathcal{P}})\big|_{\mathcal{P}}
=k-\operatorname{tr}\big(I_k-(I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big),

and the reducible variance is

k-\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big).

The numerator is

\operatorname{MMSE}_V|_{\mathcal{P}}-\operatorname{MMSE}_{VR}|_{\mathcal{P}}
=
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big).

Dividing numerator by denominator and multiplying both by -1 gives

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}}
=
\frac{
\operatorname{tr}\big((I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}})^{-1}\big)-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}{
k-\operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)
}.

∎

B.4 Schur Complement of the \mathcal{P}-Block

Partition J_{VR} in the [U,U_\perp] basis:

J_{VR}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}} & A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}\\
A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}} & I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp}
\end{pmatrix}.

The Schur complement of the lower-right block is

S
=
I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}
-
A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}
\big(I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp}\big)^{-1}
A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}}.

The \mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}-block of the inverse is \Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}=S^{-1}. Since the subtracted term is positive semidefinite,

S\preceq I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}},

and therefore

\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\succeq
\big(I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}\big)^{-1}.

The cross-block coupling inflates the posterior covariance inside \mathcal{P}, reducing \eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{P}} relative to the commuting case. The coupling term is zero iff A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}=0, which recovers the commuting calibration.

B.5 Curvature Extension (Heuristic)

When the student is a smooth nonlinear estimator manifold \mathcal{M}=\{f(\phi)\}\subset\mathbb{R}^d of dimension k, the flat subspace \mathcal{P} is replaced by the tangent space \mathcal{T}_{\theta_V^*}\mathcal{M}=\operatorname{range}(J_f(\phi_V^*)) at the V-optimal point. The extrinsic curvature enters through the second fundamental form

\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{M}}(\theta_V^*)_{ij}
=
\langle \nabla^2_{\phi_i\phi_j}f(\phi_V^*),\,\theta_{VR}^*-\theta_V^*\rangle.

In the Laplace / high-SNR regime,

\Sigma_{VR}^{\mathcal{M}}
\approx
\big(
I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{T}}+A_{R,\mathcal{T}}^{\mathrm{Schur}}+\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{M}}(\theta_V^*)
\big)^{-1}.

When \mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{M}}\succ 0, the manifold curves away from the trace-supervised target and \eta_{\mathrm{info}}^{\mathcal{M}} drops below the flat tangent-space bound. This extension is stated as a heuristic and is not used in any proposition of the main text.

