Appendix C: Proof of the Quadratic Risk Lower Bound

C.1 The PL Inequality

A differentiable f is \mu-strongly convex on a convex set \mathcal{C} if

f(y)\ge f(x)+\langle\nabla f(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{\mu}{2}\|y-x\|^2

for all x,y\in\mathcal{C}. Minimizing both sides over y gives

f(x)-f^*\le \frac{1}{2\mu}\|\nabla f(x)\|^2,

equivalently \|\nabla f(x)\|^2\ge 2\mu(f(x)-f^*). This is the Polyak–Łojasiewicz inequality implied by strong convexity. The descent lemma under L-smoothness states

f(y)\le f(x)+\langle\nabla f(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\|y-x\|^2.

C.2 Projection Inequality

Lemma C.1. At a V-stationary point \phi_V^* with \nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}_V(\phi_V^*)=0,

\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2
\ge
\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2.

Proof. Since g_{VR}=g_V+g_R and g_V(\phi_V^*)=0, we have g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)=g_R(\phi_V^*). Decompose \Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R into its component in \mathcal{K}^V and its orthogonal complement:

\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R
=
\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R
+
(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R.

The two terms are orthogonal by construction, so

\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2^2
=
\|\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2^2
+
\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2^2
\ge
\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2^2.

∎

C.3 Proof of Proposition 2

Proposition 2. Under (SA4), a projected gradient step \phi^+=\phi_V^*-\frac{1}{L}\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*) satisfies

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi^+)
\ge
\frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2L}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

If \phi_{VR}^* is the \mathcal{P}-constrained minimizer, then

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)
\ge
\frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2\mu}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

Proof. By the descent lemma with step 1/L,

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi^+)
\le
\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)
-
\frac{1}{2L}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

Rearranging,

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi^+)
\ge
\frac{1}{2L}\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

By Lemma C.1,

\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2
\ge
\|(I-\Pi_{\mathcal{K}^V})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2
=
\alpha(\phi_V^*)\nu(\phi_V^*)\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2,

where the last equality uses the definitions of \alpha and \nu at the single step \phi_V^*. Substituting gives the first bound.

For the second bound, apply the PL inequality at \phi_V^*:

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)
\ge
\frac{1}{2\mu}\|\nabla\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

Since \nabla\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)=g_R(\phi_V^*),

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)
\ge
\frac{1}{2\mu}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

The projection inequality sharpens the gradient norm to \alpha\nu\|g_R\|, giving

\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_V^*)-\mathcal{L}_{VR}(\phi_{VR}^*)
\ge
\frac{\alpha^2\nu^2}{2\mu}\|g_R(\phi_V^*)\|_2^2.

∎

C.4 Two-Layer ReLU Interpretation

For a two-layer network f_\phi(x)=m^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^m a_j\sigma(w_j^\top x) in the lazy / NTK regime, the active sign patterns \mathbf{1}\{w_j(0)^\top x>0\} are frozen at initialization. The structurally reachable subspace \mathcal{P} is the span of the active feature Jacobians. If R encodes a compositional procedure requiring a sign flip outside the initialized cones, \Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\approx 0 and \alpha\approx 0. If R’s target residual lies inside active cones not spanned by V’s labels, then \alpha\nu>0 and the PL bound guarantees a strictly positive risk reduction of order \alpha^2\nu^2. The bound is conditional on the local strong-convexity assumption; it is not a global statement.

