Appendix D: Derivation of the Pass@K Crossing

D.1 Per-Prompt Advantage

For a prompt x,

G_K(A\mid x)=1-\big(1-p_A(x)\big)^K.

The per-prompt advantage of (V,R) over V is

\Delta_K(x)
=
\big(1-p_V(x)\big)^K-\big(1-p_{VR}(x)\big)^K.

D.2 Derivative with Respect to Continuous Budget

Treat K as continuous and differentiate:

\frac{\partial\Delta_K(x)}{\partial K}
=
\big(1-p_V\big)^K\ln(1-p_V)
-
\big(1-p_{VR}\big)^K\ln(1-p_{VR}).

On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}, p_V>p_{VR}, so \ln(1-p_V)<\ln(1-p_{VR})<0 and the first term dominates at small K. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}, p_{VR}>p_V\approx 0, so \ln(1-p_{VR})<\ln(1-p_V)\approx 0 and the second term dominates at large K.

D.3 Proof of Proposition 3

Proposition 3. Under the partition \mathcal{T}=\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}\cup\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}},

\lim_{K\to\infty}\big(G_K(V,R)-G_K(V)\big)=\pi_{\mathrm{tail}},

and the crossover budget is

B^*
\approx
\frac{1}{\epsilon}
\ln\!\left(
\frac{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}}
{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}
\right).

Proof. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}, (1-p_V)^K\to 0 as K\to\infty because p_V>0. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}, (1-p_{VR})^K\to 0 because p_{VR}>0. The limiting advantage is therefore

\lim_{K\to\infty}\Delta_K(x)=
\begin{cases}
0, & x\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}},\\
1, & x\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}},
\end{cases}

and taking expectations gives \pi_{\mathrm{tail}}.

For the crossover, expand G_K(V,R)-G_K(V)=0 to first order in the tail rate \epsilon around K=B^*. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{tail}}, 1-(1-p_{VR})^K\approx 1-e^{-\epsilon K}. On \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}, the loss relative to V is approximately \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}] at small K. Setting the two contributions equal,

\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}\big(1-e^{-\epsilon B^*}\big)
=
\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}].

Solving for B^*,

e^{-\epsilon B^*}
=
1-\frac{\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}}
=
\frac{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}},

hence

B^*=\frac{1}{\epsilon}
\ln\!\left(
\frac{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}}
{\pi_{\mathrm{tail}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{mod}}}[p_V-p_{VR}]}
\right).

∎

D.4 TAS@K

The Teacher Advantage Score at K is computed before training from the teacher’s and student’s output distributions. For a candidate teacher T and student S,

\mathrm{TAS}@K(T,S)
=
\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{T}}
\left[
\mathrm{pass}@K\big(T\mid x\big)
-
\mathrm{pass}@K\big(S\mid x\big)
\right].

The trace-versus-visible analogue replaces T with the trace-conditioned distribution and S with the V-conditioned distribution. The score is used only to pre-register the expected sign of d\eta/dB; it is not a component of any proposition in the main text. The OPD test-time scaling study reports that TAS@K ordering agrees with observed pass@K improvements in 83.6\% of experiments across thirteen benchmarks.

