Appendix E: Le Cam Deficiency and the Risk Bound

E.1 One-Sided Deficiency

For experiments \mathcal{E}=\{P_\theta\} and \mathcal{F}=\{Q_\theta\} on the same parameter space,

\delta(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})
=
\inf_{M}\sup_{\theta\in\Theta}
\|MP_\theta-Q_\theta\|_{\mathrm{TV}},

where M ranges over Markov kernels. \delta(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F}) is the smallest worst-case total-variation error incurred by any randomized reconstruction of \mathcal{F} from \mathcal{E}.

E.2 Proof of Proposition 4

Proposition 4. If G(\phi)=M_\ell-\mathbb{E}[\ell(\Theta,\phi)] with \ell\in[0,M_\ell], then for any estimator \phi,

G\big|_{\mathcal{E}_{VR}}(\phi)-G\big|_{\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)}}(\phi)
\le
M_\ell\,\delta\big(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}\big).

Consequently,

\eta_{\mathrm{info}}(\lambda)
\le
\alpha\nu\,
\frac{M_\ell\,\delta\big(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}\big)}
{G^{\mathrm{oracle}}_{\mathcal{S}}(V,R)-G_{\mathcal{S}}(\varnothing)}.

Proof. For any Markov kernel M,

\big|G\big|_{\mathcal{E}_{VR}}(\phi)-G\big|_{\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)}}(\phi)\big|
=
\big|\mathbb{E}_{P_\theta^{V,R}}[\ell(\Theta,\phi)]
-
\mathbb{E}_{P_\theta^{V(\lambda)}}[\ell(\Theta,\phi)]\big|.

Since \ell/M_\ell\in[0,1], the expectation difference is bounded by M_\ell times the total-variation distance between the induced distributions of \Theta. For any coupling of the two experiments,

\big|\mathbb{E}_{P}[\ell]-\mathbb{E}_{Q}[\ell]\big|
\le
M_\ell\,\|P-Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Taking the infimum over Markov kernels M gives M_\ell\,\delta(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}). The information share is the difference in oracle capability between the full and leaked experiments, so

G^{\mathrm{oracle}}_{\mathcal{S}}(V,R)-G^{\mathrm{oracle}}_{\mathcal{S}}(V(\lambda))
\le
M_\ell\,\delta(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}).

Dividing by the denominator and multiplying by the reachability attenuation \alpha\nu from the main-text bound gives the stated result. ∎

E.3 Erasure Channel and Pinsker

For the erasure channel where V(\lambda) reveals R with probability \lambda and erases to the prior with probability 1-\lambda, the optimal kernel imputes the prior upon erasure. The deficiency is linear:

\delta\big(\mathcal{E}_{V(\lambda)},\mathcal{E}_{VR}\big)
=
(1-\lambda)\sup_\theta
\big\|
P_\theta^V\otimes P^R-P_\theta^{V,R}
\big\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
=
(1-\lambda)\delta_0.

Pinsker’s inequality gives

\|P-Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}^2
\le
\frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|Q),

so

\delta_0
\le
\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}I(R;\Theta\mid V)}.

Substituting recovers the mutual-information bound in the main text as a special case. The bound is O(\delta) and is loose; it is an upper bound, not a tight characterization.

