Appendix F: Estimation of \alpha and \nu

F.1 Sketched Subspace Tracking

Algorithm F.1 (online PCA / sketched SVD for \mathcal{K}^V_t).

· Maintain a sketch S_t\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r} of recent V-supervised gradients, with r the sketch rank.
· At each step, update S_t by exponential forgetting: S_t\leftarrow \gamma S_{t-1}+(1-\gamma)g_V(x_t) for a forgetting factor \gamma\in(0,1).
· Compute a rank-r_V truncated SVD S_t\approx \widehat{U}_t\widehat{\Sigma}_t\widehat{V}_t^\top.
· Set \widehat{\mathcal{K}}^V_t=\operatorname{span}(\widehat{U}_{t,:r_V}), with r_V chosen by an energy threshold \sum_{i\le r_V}\widehat{\sigma}_i^2/\sum_i\widehat{\sigma}_i^2\ge 1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{svd}}.

The rank cutoff r_V must be pre-registered and reported alongside \alpha,\nu. A rank that scales with batch size makes \nu partly a measure of batch size over parameter dimension; the energy threshold avoids this by selecting directions rather than counting gradients.

F.2 Estimators

For a matched batch at step t,

\widehat{\alpha}_t
=
\frac{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2}{\|g_R\|_2},
\qquad
\widehat{\nu}_t
=
\frac{\|(I-\Pi_{\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^V_t})\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2}
{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}}g_R\|_2}.

Report time-averaged values \widehat{\alpha}=T^{-1}\sum_t\widehat{\alpha}_t, \widehat{\nu}=T^{-1}\sum_t\widehat{\nu}_t, with bootstrap confidence intervals over batches and training seeds.

F.3 Null Control

Run the same estimator on pure-noise “traces” g_{\mathrm{noise}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2I). The null values \widehat{\alpha}_{\mathrm{null}},\widehat{\nu}_{\mathrm{null}} calibrate the estimator’s bias. A reported \widehat{\alpha} is interpreted only relative to this null; a value statistically indistinguishable from \widehat{\alpha}_{\mathrm{null}} is treated as \alpha\approx 0.

F.4 Layerwise Profile

The scalar \alpha averages over layers. Report the layerwise profile

\alpha^{(l)}
=
\mathbb{E}_t\left[
\frac{\|\Pi_{\mathcal{P}^{(l)}}g_R^{(l)}\|}{\|g_R^{(l)}\|}
\right]

for each layer l. The Low-Rank Clone result indicates that the unreachable fraction is layer-dependent: LRC deploys a full-width student MLP but ties training to a teacher-induced slice, leaving 62.5\%–81.4\% of each deployed matrix’s independent linear degrees of freedom unreachable, and training on the entire deployed matrix recovers +2.36/+2.71/+10.45 Avg9 across three teachers at fixed deployed shape. The layerwise profile makes the location of the reachability gap visible rather than averaging it into a single number.

