Appendix H: Supporting Lemmas

H.1 Data Processing Inequality

If U\to W\to Z is a Markov chain, then I(U;Z)\le I(U;W). Applied to \Theta\to(X,V)\to R_{\mathrm{null}}, this gives I(R_{\mathrm{null}};\Theta\mid X,V)=0 when R_{\mathrm{null}} is generated from (X,V) alone.

H.2 Schur Complement Identity

For a block matrix

M=\begin{pmatrix}A & B\\ C & D\end{pmatrix}

with D invertible, the top-left block of M^{-1} is (A-BD^{-1}C)^{-1}. This is used in Appendix B.4 with A=I_k+A_{V,\mathcal{P}}+A_{R,\mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}}, B=A_{R,\mathcal{P}\perp}, C=A_{R,\perp\mathcal{P}}, D=I_{d-k}+A_{V,\perp}+A_{R,\perp\perp}.

H.3 Descent Lemma

If f is L-smooth on a convex set containing x and y, then

f(y)\le f(x)+\langle\nabla f(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\|y-x\|^2.

Applied with y=x-\frac{1}{L}\nabla f(x) gives f(y)\le f(x)-\frac{1}{2L}\|\nabla f(x)\|^2.

H.4 Pinsker’s Inequality

For probability measures P,Q,

\|P-Q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}^2\le \frac{1}{2}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|Q).

Used in Appendix E.3 to relate the deficiency to mutual information.

H.5 Cauchy–Schwarz for Projections

For orthogonal projections \Pi_1,\Pi_2 with \Pi_2\preceq\Pi_1, \|\Pi_2 x\|\le\|\Pi_1 x\|. Used in Lemma C.1.

